    "_notes": "Real backup of the user\u0027s actual garden, exported directly from garden-planner-dev\u0027s live Postgres (garden/bed/planting/bed_equipment tables) on 2026-07-23 via a one-off SQL extraction, not generated by data/etl/generate_example_garden.py the way data/example_garden.json is. Named \u0027De Heuvel\u0027 by the user in the app itself (started from the seeded example-garden layout, then personalized: renamed, real climate_zone/location/orientation_deg set, beds repositioned/rotated to match the user\u0027s real physical layout - several bed notes/plant choices below still carry the original ETL-generated fixture text since those specific beds weren\u0027t edited). Unlike example_garden.json\u0027s plantings (flat bed-local x_cm/y_cm points, deliberately simplified per import_example_garden.py\u0027s own docstring), this file preserves each planting\u0027s REAL geometry (width/height/rotation) as recorded, and also captures Garden-level fields (name/border_geometry/climate_zone/location/orientation_deg) that example_garden.json\u0027s schema has no place for at all. IMPORTANT RESTORE CAVEAT: the existing importer (backend/app/scripts/import_example_garden.py) does NOT currently support this file\u0027s richer shape - it only reads a flat beds[]-with-x_cm/y_cm-points structure, and its import_garden() only ever creates a Garden with a bounding-box default (never restores a real name/climate/location/orientation, and never overwrites an existing Garden row). Restoring this backup exactly (not just approximately) needs either a new/extended importer aware of this file\u0027s shape, or manual reconstruction via the UI using this file as a reference. Not wired into any deploy/import skill - this is a point-in-time snapshot, regenerate manually via the same SQL extraction if the real garden changes again.",
